      "year": 2020,
      "abstract": "actions of powerful states. And the idea of perpetual peace is a more particular notion than the simple conception of a peaceful life (stigmatized by Kant as \u201ctranquil indolence\u201d or \u201cpastoral existence\u201d), one that depends on the idea of the full development of human beings\u2019 various natural talents and endowments (106). All these notions may require a more severe moral discipline and more constrained political options, and are consequently less universalistic than has often been claimed. That Kant provides us with uniquely fruitful methods for deriving contemporary political norms is a claim that requires more justification than it gets here. But Huseyinzadegan is surely right that what Kant is attempting in the postcritical writings differs in kind from the critiques. Furthermore, it seems correct that the ideas he offers there are not directly derivable from, or justifiable by, his more formal notions of knowledge, morality, or law. For these points alone, this book should prove an extremely valuable corrective to standard views of Kant\u2019s legacy. O m a r D a h b o u r Hunter College and Graduate School, City University of New York",
