      "year": 2020,
      "abstract": "generally refrains from engaging with naturalism and anthropology in historical terms. Her philosophical aim is neither to explore Herder\u2019s novel notions of force, organism, life, and sensibility within eighteenth-century anthropology, nor to reconstruct the genesis and deeper unity of Herder\u2019s philosophy, however interesting such inquiry might be. This also explains her selective bibliography, more thorough on aesthetics than on Enlightenment anthropology. Zuckert\u2019s intention is to spell out Herder\u2019s methodological affinities with twenty-first-century naturalism and the fruitfulness of such naturalism for aesthetics. This is a legitimate restriction of her viewpoint, which gives her book a clear focus. However, it also leads to certain ambiguities and imprecisions. Zuckert could have provided a more complete account of Herder\u2019s double perspective, which is both naturalist and theist, as presented in his treatise on the Origin of Language. This would have allowed her to take a more nuanced view on humans who are never fully part of the natural world, as she claims (11), but also, constitutively, remain \u201cdivine\u201d animals or \u201cwanderers\u201d between two worlds, the natural and the supernatural. Does the aesthetic experience not involve some elevation toward the \u201cdivine\u201d? Does it not offer some empirical \u201cself-knowledge\u201d and experience of humanity and of the very sensate, cognitive, and aesthetic faculties human beings possess, different from the biological growth and flourishing characteristic of plants and nonhuman animals? Zuckert\u2019s position would have been strengthened by a clearer description of German rationalist aesthetics (which is, admittedly, misrepresented in the older studies quoted, such as B\u00e4umler\u2019s). She, of course, persuasively presents some major consequences of Herder\u2019s novel aesthetics and shift of emphasis from imitation or representation to sensibility, for the debate on art or Sch\u00f6ne K\u00fcnste. But a closer glance at the German rationalists might have allowed her to better spell out their primary and common aim: a reinvention of sensibility. Their concern is not with art for its own sake but with sensibility (reason and humanity), to be rethought within a Leibnizian and Wolffian framework (different from British empiricism), and with the aid of art and artistic models. Manifestly, Herder, who early on engages in a deep debate with Wolff, Baumgarten, and Lessing, not forgetting Mendelssohn, Sulzer, and Winckelmann (who all deserved greater attention!), is continuing this aesthetics project, thereby illustrating its tremendous significance for eighteenth-century German philosophy. S t e f a n i e B u c h e n a u Universit\u00e9 Paris 8 Saint-Denis",
